/*!
\page page_crysp_specification CrysP-CLI Specification

[TOC]

This page provides the full specification of the inputs and outputs required by each CrysP-CLI module.

<HR>

\section sec_spec_generic Generic configuration
Each \ref sec_format_files_main starts with a generic configuration section, applicable to all CrysP-CLI modules, which describes the multiscale model used for the simulation:

-# \confdef{texture_file, \ref sec_format_fields_string} specifies the (relative) path to the input texture file. This file represents the texture of the material as a discrete set of grain orientations. Its format is described in the \ref sec_format_files_orientation section.

-# \confdef{model_type, \ref sec_format_fields_string} specifies the multi-scale crystal plasticity model to be used in the calculations. The supported models are:
    - **ALAMEL**: Advanced LAMEL model developed at KU Leuven. If this model is chosen, the following suboption must be provided:
        - \confdef{microstructure_file,\ref sec_format_fields_string} Relative path to the microstrucure file. It describes the microstructure of the material, as a discrete set of grain boundary normals. It is formatted a an \ref sec_format_files_orientation.

      The ALAMEL model will process the texture and microstrucure files sequentially. Each pair of consecutive grain orientations is matched with the topmost boundary normal that has not yet been used to form a cluster. If the microstrucure file does not contain enough boundary normals to process all grain orientations, it is reprocessed sequentially as needed. For example, if the texture consists of 800 grain orientations, but the microstructure file contains only 250 boundary normals, ALAMEL will construct 250 clusters with the first 500 grain orientations and each of the boundary normals, and 150 clusters with the last 300 grain orientations and the first 150 boundary normals, all in sequential order. Note that often it is not possible to experimentally obtain the microstructure of the sample. In that case, it is recommended to generate a set of boundary normals programmatically that corresponds with the average grain morphology.
    - **FCTaylor**: Traditional full constraints Taylor model.

-# \confdef{slip_system_set, \ref sec_format_fields_string} specifies the slip system set to be used. Valid values are:
        - **fcc12**: FCC crystal with 12 slip systems of {111}&lt;110&gt; type.
        - **bcc24**: BCC crystal with 12 slip systems of {110}&lt;111&gt; type, plus 12 slip systems of {112}&lt;111&gt; type.
        - **bcc48**: BCC crystal with 12 bcc slip systems of {110}&lt;111&gt; type, plus 12 slip systems of {112}&lt;111&gt; type, plus 24 slip systems of {123}&lt;111&gt; type.


-# \confdef{hardening_model,\ref sec_format_fields_integer} specifies the micro-scale hardening law used for the simulation. The hardening law maps the critical resolved shear stress \f$ \tau_c \f$ of the slip systems one some set of internal state variables. These internal state variables usually require configuration as well, and are specified as a configuration option directly after the hardening law identifier. The supported hardening laws and their corresponding configuration parameters are the following:

      <HR>
      - **0**: No hardening occurs. \f$ \tau_c \f$ on each slip system is set to 1.0. This hardening law requires no configuration suboptions.
      <HR>
      - **1**: 2-stage Voce hardening law. It is an isotropic, phenomonological hardening law which describes \f$ \tau_c \f$ on each of the slip systems as:
        \f[ \begin{split}
			& \tau_c = \tau^{III}_{s} - (\tau^{III}_{s}-\tau_0) \exp{\left( \frac{-\theta_0}{\tau^{III}_{s}-\tau_0}\gamma \right)} \quad \textrm{for} \quad \gamma\leq\gamma_T \\
			& \tau_c = \tau^{IV}_{s} - (\tau^{IV}_{s}-\tau_T) \exp{\left( \frac{-\theta_T}{\tau^{IV}_{s}-\tau_T}(\gamma-\gamma_T) \right)} \quad \textrm{for} \quad \gamma\geq\gamma_T
		\end{split} \f]
        With:
        <div style="margin-left:2em;">
            - \f$ \tau_0 \f$: initial flow stress (i.e. of the stage `III`),
            - \f$ \tau^{III}_{s} \f$: saturation flow stress associated to stage `III`,
            - \f$ \tau^{IV}_{s} \f$: saturation flow stress associated to stage `IV`,
            - \f$ \theta_0 \f$: initial hardening rate (i.e. of the stage `III`),
            - \f$ \theta_T \f$: hardening rate of the transition point between stages `III` and `IV`,
            - \f$ \gamma \f$: Accumulated slip across all slip systems,
            - \f$ \gamma_T \f$: Transition point between stages III and IV in terms of accumulated slip.
        </div>
        \f$ \gamma \f$ is a state variable that is updated throughout the simulation. \f$ \gamma_T \f$ is a dependent hardening parameter calculated internally during initialization. The other parameters are independent and must be provided through a configuration suboption of type (\ref sec_format_options_real_array[5]): \f$ \tau_0 \space \tau^{III}_{s} \space \tau^{IV}_{s} \space \theta_0 \space \theta_T \f$.
        The input parameters are subject to the constraints \f$ 0 < \tau_0 < \tau^{III}_{s} \leq \tau^{IV}_{s} \f$ and \f$ 0 < \theta_T < \theta_0 \f$.
		\note The one-stage Voce variant is retrieved by imposing \f$ \tau^{III}_{s} = \tau^{IV}_{s} \f$ and setting \f$ \theta_T \f$ to an arbitrary value within constraint boundaries.

        <HR>
	  - **2**: Hockett-Sherby hardening. It is an isotropic, phenomonological hardening law which describes \f$ \tau_c \f$ on each of the slip systems as:
        \f[ \tau_c = \tau_s - (\tau_s - \tau_0) \exp{(-b \gamma^n)}  \f]
        With:
        <div style="margin-left:2em;">
            - \f$ \tau_0 \f$: Initial flow stress,
            - \f$ \tau_s \f$: Saturation flow stress,
            - \f$ b \f$: Hardening exponent,
            - \f$ \gamma \f$: Accumulated slip across all slip systems,
            - \f$ n \f$: Hardening exponent on the accumulated slip.
        </div>
        \f$ \gamma \f$ is a state variable that is updated throughout the simulation. The other parameters are independent and must be provided through a configuration suboption of type (\ref sec_format_options_real_array[4]): \f$ \tau_0 \space \tau_{s} \space b \space n \f$.
        <HR>
      - `3` Swift hardening law. It is an isotropic, phenomonological hardening law which describes \f$ \tau_c \f$ on each of the slip systems as:
		\f[	\tau_c = \tau_0 \left(\frac{\gamma}{\gamma_0} + 1 \right)^n \f]
        With:
        <div style="margin-left:2em;">
            - \f$ \tau_0 \f$: Initial flow stress,
            - \f$ \gamma \f$: Accumulated slip across all slip systems,
            - \f$ \gamma_0 \f$: Initial accumulated slip,
            - \f$ n \f$: Hardening exponent.
        </div>
        \f$ \gamma \f$ is a state variable that is updated throughout the simulation. The other parameters are independent and must be provided through a configuration suboption of type (\ref sec_format_options_real_array[3]): \f$ \tau_0 \space \gamma_0 \space n \f$.
		Only positive values of parameters are acceptable (\f$ 0 < \tau_0 \f$, \f$ 0 < \gamma_0 \f$ and \f$ 0 < n \f$).
        <HR>
      - `11` Dislocation Substructural Hardening (DSH) model, edge variant. This is a physics-based hardening model developed at KU Leuven for specific steels. It is described in _Peeters et al. ActaMat 49: 1607-19_. The parameters for this model (as described in _Peeters et al. ActaMat 49: 1607-19_) are:

        <div style="margin-left:2em;">
            - \f$ b \f$
            - \f$ G \f$
            - \f$ \alpha \f$
            - \f$ f \f$
            - \f$ \tau_0 \f$
            - \f$ I \f$
            - \f$ R \f$
            - \f$ I_{wd} \f$
            - \f$ R_{wd} \f$
            - \f$ R_{ncg} \f$
            - \f$ \beta_1 \f$
            - \f$ \beta_2 \f$
            - \f$ I_{wp} \f$
            - \f$ R_{wp} \f$
            - \f$ R_{rev} \f$
            - \f$ R_2 \f$
        </div>
        The parameters are independent and must be provided through a configuration suboption of type \ref sec_format_options_real_array[16].
        \attention This hardening model only supports the bcc24 slip system family.

        <HR>
      - `12` Dislocation Substructural Hardening (DSH) model, screw variant. This is a physics-based hardening model developed at KU Leuven for specific steels. It is based on the DSH model described in _Peeters et al. ActaMat 49: 1607-19_, but assumes screw dislocations instead of edge dislocations. The parameters for this model are identical to those for the edge variant.
        \attention This hardening model only supports the bcc24 slip system family.

        <HR>
      - `13` Dislocation Substructural Hardening (DSH) model, loop variant. Analogous to 12, but for loop dislocations instead of screw dislocations.
        \attention This hardening model only supports the bcc24 slip system family.

        <HR>

\par Example:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~{.txt}
        #Example multilevel model configuration: ALAMEL simulation of an fcc12 material with voce hardening.
        example_texture.smt        #Path to texture file
        ALAMEL                     #Mesoscale model
        equiaxed.smt               #Path to microstructure file
        fcc12                      #Slip system family
        1                          # Hardening law Id
        12.39 15.0 20.0 0.2 0.1    # Hardening parameter set
~~~~~~~~~~~~~


<HR>



Module-Specific configuration
==================
Each CrysP-CLI module has its own module-specific configuration, which is appended to the configuration file directly after the general configuration options.
Furthermore, each module will generate its own specific output data. All of this module-specific specification is grouped here.


<HR>

\section module_asr Multiaxial Stress Test Module (keyword: ASR)
The Multiaxial Stress Test Module prescribes a generic stress loading on a virtual sample.
Several subsequent deformation _steps_ may be imposed;
each step imposes a certain (constant) stress mode for a certain deformation (which may be infinitesimally small).

A specific _sample reference frame_ (with `x`-, `y`- and `z`-axes) is associated to the virtual sample.
The imposed stress state is described with reference to this sample reference frame.

\subsection configuration_asr Module-specific configuration

-# \confdef{n_steps, \ref sec_format_fields_integer} sets the number of subsequent deformation steps, each one being specified by the configuration settings 3.-4. and their optional subsettings.
These settings are repeated \conffield{n_steps} times in configuration file, in the same order as they are to be applied to the virtual sample.
-# \confdef{stress_mode, \ref sec_format_options_symmatrix} specifies the imposed stress mode for given step, with reference to the sample (`xyz`) reference frame, and in format specified in \ref symsrtensor_option.
Note that the hydrostatic part of the imposed stress mode is ignored, as well as its norm.
-# \confdef{step_magnitude,real} The Von Mises equivalent true strain to be achieved in this step. If it is 0, the strain mode corresponding to this stress state is querried, without updating the material state.



\par Example
\includelineno example_asr_config.cfg

\subsection output_asr Module-specific output

The \ref sec_format_out_main contains the following fields:
<ol>
	<li> \outdef{epsilon_vM, eps_vM_ASR} The von Mises equivalent strain of the current step
	\f$ \epsilon_{vM,step} \f$

	It is derived from the strain tensor of current step \f$ \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{step} \f$ as:
	\f[
		\epsilon_{vM,total}=\sqrt{2/3} \| \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{total} \|
	\f]
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{sigma_vM} The von Mises equivalent stress  of the current increment. It is derived from the deviatoric stress tensor as follows:
	\f[
		\sigma_{vM,step}=\sqrt{3/2} \| \boldsymbol{\sigma}_{step} \|
	\f]
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{dotW} The rate of plastic work \f$ \dot{W} \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{residual} The norm of the difference between the stress mode requested by the user and the stress mode obtained by the model.
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{sigma_11} The xx-component of the Cauchy stress tensor \f$ \mathbf{S} \f$
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{sigma_22} Similar to \ref sigma_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{sigma_33} Similar to \ref sigma_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{sigma_23} Similar to \ref sigma_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{sigma_13} Similar to \ref sigma_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{sigma_12} Similar to \ref sigma_11
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{A_11} The xx-component of the strain mode tensor \f$ \mathbf{A} \f$
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_22} Similar to \ref A_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_33} Similar to \ref A_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_23} Similar to \ref A_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_13} Similar to \ref A_11
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_12} Similar to \ref A_11
	</li>
</ol>

\par Example
\includelineno example_asr_output.csv

Additionally, this module generates a \ref sec_format_out_texture. The texture is reported at the end of each deformation step.

<HR>

\section module_qrs Uniaxial Anisotropy Module (keyword: QRS)

The Uniaxial Anisotropy Module provides Lankford coefficients (in two variants: q-values and r-values; cf. below) and normalized uniaxial yield stresses (s-values),
for a specified range of in-plane directions, and under tensile loading. These properties are queried for the virtual microstructure in the given state without
any finite deformation imposed. There is no evolution of state associated with this module.

For each loading case, a _sample reference frame_ (with `x`-, `y`- and `z`-axes) is associated to the virtual sample.
The loading direction is aligned with the sample `x`-axis, by convention.
The `x`- and `y`-axes are restricted to the material `1`-`2`-plane.
Consequently, the sample `z`-axis is aligned with the material `3`-direction.

\subsection configuration_qrs Module-specific configuration

-# \confdef{angular_resolution, \ref sec_format_fields_real} defines the angle in degrees between consecutive sampling directions in the xy-plane.

\par Example
\includelineno example_qrs_config.cfg

\subsection output_qrs Module-specific output

The \ref sec_format_out_main contains the following fields:
<ol>
	<li> \outdef{angle} The in-plane orientation angle in degrees.
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{q-value} The instantaneous q-value \f$ q_i \f$

	It is defined as
	\f[
		q_i = \frac{-D_{yy}}{D_{xx}}
	\f]
	where \f$D_{yy}\f$ and \f$D_{xx}\f$ are the strain rates in the width and tensile
	directions, respectively. It relates to the instantaneous r-value as \f$ q_i = \frac{r_i}{1+r_i} \f$.
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{r-value} The instantaneous r-value \f$ r_i \f$

	It is defined as
	\f[
		r_i = \frac{D_{yy}}{D_{zz}}
	\f]
	where \f$D_{yy}\f$ and \f$D_{zz}\f$ are the strain rates in the width and thickness
	directions, respectively. It relates to the instantaneous q-value as
	\f$ \displaystyle r_i = \frac{q_i}{1-q_i} \f$.
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{s-value} The norm of the deviatoric stress tensor in the tested direction.
	</li>

	<li> \outref{residual}
	</li>
</ol>

\par Example
\includelineno example_qrs_output.csv

<HR>

\section module_yld Yield Locus Module (keyword: YLD)

The Yield Locus Module calculates generic 2-dimensional sections of the yield locus.
There is no evolution of state associated with this module.

\subsection configuration_yld Module-specific configuration

-# \confdef{angular_resolution, \ref sec_format_fields_real} Angle between sample points, expressed in degrees and measured from the 1st section base vector towards the 2nd section base vector.
-# \confdef{base_sigma_X,\ref sec_format_options_symmatrix} is the base tensor along the X-axis of the yield locus section.
-# \confdef{base_sigma_Y,\ref sec_format_options_symmatrix} is the base tensor along the Y-axis of the yield locus section. It must be orthogonal to \conffield{base_sigma_X}.

\par Example
\includelineno example_yld_config.cfg

\subsection output_yld Module-specific output

The \ref sec_format_out_main contains the following fields:
<ol>
	<li> \outdef{angle,yldout_theta} The angle \f$ \theta \f$ corresponding to a yield locus
	point, as requested in \conffield{theta_range}, expressed in degrees.
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{stress} The norm of the Cauchy stress tensor (i.e. the radial coordinate of the yield stress) at the yield locus point.
	</li>
    <li> \outref{A_11}
    </li>
    <li> \outref{A_22}
    </li>
    <li> \outref{A_33}
    </li>
    <li> \outref{A_23}
    </li>
    <li> \outref{A_13}
    </li>
    <li> \outref{A_12}
    </li>
	<li> \outref{residual}
	</li>
</ol>

\par Example
\includelineno example_yld_output.csv

<HR>

\section module_adp Arbirtary Deformation Path (keyword: ADP)

The Arbitrary Deformation Path prescribes an arbitrary strain-driven deformation on a virtual
sample. Several subsequent deformation _steps_ may be imposed; each step imposes a certain
(constant) velocity gradient.

\subsection configuration_adp Module-specific configuration

-# \confdef{n_steps,\ref sec_format_fields_integer} sets the number of subsequent deformation steps,
   each one being specified by later configuration settings and their optional
   subsettings. These subsettings are repeated \conffield{n_steps} times in configuration
   file, in the same order as they are to be applied to the virtual sample:
    -# \confdef{Velocity_gradient, \ref sec_format_options_matrix} Velocity gradient tensor.

    -# \confdef{strain_magnitude, \ref sec_format_fields_real} Magnitude of the von-mises equivalent logarihmic strain to be reached in this step. If it is set to 0, the material response to the imposed deformation mode is calculated without updating the material state.

---

\par Example
\includelineno example_adp_config.cfg

\subsection output_adp Module-specific output

The \ref sec_format_out_main contains the following fields:
-# \outdef{duration} The duration of the increment.

-# \outdef{eps_vM} Von Mises strain at the end of the increment.

-# \outdef{L_11} The 11 component of the velocity gradient tensor \f$ \mathbf{L} \f$ imposed
   during the increment, given in the material (123) reference frame.
-# \outdef{L_22} similar to \ref L_11
-# \outdef{L_33} similar to \ref L_11
-# \outdef{L_12} similar to \ref L_11
-# \outdef{L_23} similar to \ref L_11
-# \outdef{L_31} similar to \ref L_11
-# \outdef{L_21} similar to \ref L_11
-# \outdef{L_32} similar to \ref L_11
-# \outdef{L_13} similar to \ref L_11

-# \outdef{S_11,adp_S_11} The 11 component of the deviatoric stress.
-# \outdef{S_22,adp_S_22} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"
-# \outdef{S_33,adp_S_33} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"
-# \outdef{S_12,adp_S_12} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"
-# \outdef{S_23,adp_S_23} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"
-# \outdef{S_13,adp_S_13} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"

-# \outdef{M} Taylor factor during the increment.

\par Example
\includelineno example_adp_output.csv

Additionally, this module generates a \ref sec_format_out_texture. The texture is reported at the end of each deformation step.
*/
